{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.15","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## AIM: Coding Attention Mechanism \n\n## DOB: 27-10-2026\n\n## Author: Shalu Singh","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## We will implement 4 different varients of attention mechansim\n\n![image.png](attachment:5300c3a5-40e9-4638-856a-e1edcb1a2596.png)\n","metadata":{},"attachments":{"5300c3a5-40e9-4638-856a-e1edcb1a2596.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 1) Simple Self Attention\n\n- Here we will implement self-attention without **trainable weights**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Q1) What is the goal of self-attention ?\n\nAns) The goal of self-attention is to compute the **context vector** for each input element in the  input sentence.\n\n![image.png](attachment:909b1ecd-3c95-4ec8-8bd7-7163d5fc53bf.png)\n\n","metadata":{},"attachments":{"909b1ecd-3c95-4ec8-8bd7-7163d5fc53bf.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q.2) What is a Context Vector?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. A context vector is a representation of a token that contains **information about the token itself as well as its surrounding context in the input sequence**.\n\n2. Unlike a basic embedding, which represents a token without considering the other tokens in the current input sequence, a context vector is **enriched embedding vector**.\n\n---\n\n**Example:**\n\nConsider the sentence:\n\n> \"The animal didn't cross the street because it was tired.\"\n\nThe meaning of the word *\"it\"* depends on the surrounding words. A context-aware representation can incorporate information from the sentence to help the model understand what *\"it\"* refers to.\n\n---\n\n**💡 Key Takeaway**\n\n> An embedding represents a token, while a context vector represents a token enriched with information from its context.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q.3) What Are the Algorithmic Steps to Calculate the Context Vector?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. **Compute the Attention Scores**\n2. **Compute the Attention Weights**\n3. **Compute the Context Vector**\n\n---\n\n![image.png](attachment:14284957-b4fe-4334-8d31-8c6be471ef7f.png)","metadata":{},"attachments":{"14284957-b4fe-4334-8d31-8c6be471ef7f.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Input sentence\n\nimport torch\ninputs = torch.tensor(\n [[0.43, 0.15, 0.89], # Your (x^1)\n [0.55, 0.87, 0.66], # journey (x^2)\n [0.57, 0.85, 0.64], # starts (x^3)\n [0.22, 0.58, 0.33], # with (x^4)\n [0.77, 0.25, 0.10], # one (x^5)\n [0.05, 0.80, 0.55]]) # step (x^6)\n\nprint(inputs.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:28.148225Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:28.148553Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.563596Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:28.148525Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.562755Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"torch.Size([6, 3])\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 1. Computation of Attention Scores\n\n**Answer:**\n\nThe computation of attention scores involves the following steps:\n\n1. **Dot Product of Query and Key Vectors:** Calculate the dot product between each token's **Query vector and every token's Key vector**, including its own. This determines how relevant each token is to the token being processed.\n\n   Mathematically,\n\n   $$\n   s_{ij} = \\mathbf{q}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{k}_j\n   $$\n\n   Where:\n   - $s_{ij}$ = Attention score between query token $i$ and key token $j$\n   - $\\mathbf{q}_i$ = Query vector of token $i$\n   - $\\mathbf{k}_j$ = Key vector of token $j$\n   - $\\cdot$ = Dot product operation\n\n2. **Shape of the Attention Score Matrix:** The attention score matrix is a square matrix with dimensions **n × n**, where **n** is the number of tokens in the input sequence.\n\n   Mathematically,\n\n   $$\n   \\mathbf{S} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}\n   $$\n\n3. **Dot Product as a Measure of Similarity:** The dot product measures how closely two vectors are aligned. A higher dot product indicates a greater degree of alignment or similarity between the vectors.\n\n4. **Role in Self-Attention:** In self-attention, the dot product determines how much attention each token should pay to every other token in the input sequence.\n\n---\n\n![image.png](attachment:fcd774c8-f720-4317-ba83-f4acb86cd331.png)","metadata":{},"attachments":{"fcd774c8-f720-4317-ba83-f4acb86cd331.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Example: computing attention score of 'journey' with respect to all input embeddings\n\nquery = inputs[1] # journey\nattention_score_2 = torch.empty(size = (inputs.shape[0],)) # size = total inputs in a sentence\nfor idx,ip in enumerate(inputs):\n    attention_score_2[idx] = torch.dot(query,inputs[idx]) # = query @ inputs[idx]\n\nprint(attention_score_2)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.56526Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.565662Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.717542Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.565637Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.716614Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"tensor([0.9544, 1.4950, 1.4754, 0.8434, 0.7070, 1.0865])\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 2. Computation of Attention Weights\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. **Normalization of Attention Scores:** Attention weight computation basically means normalizing the attention scores.\n\n2. **Goal of Normalization:** The main goal of normalization is to obtain attention scores that sum up to 1. This is useful for interpretation and managing training stability in LLMs.\n\n   Mathematically, for each query token $i$:\n\n   $$\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\alpha_{ij} = 1\n   $$\n\n   Where:\n   - $\\alpha_{ij}$ = Attention weight assigned by query token $i$ to key token $j$\n   - $n$ = Total number of tokens in the input sequence\n\n3. **Direction of Normalization:** Normalization is performed across each row of the attention score matrix in the standard self-attention formulation.\n\n   - **Why row-wise normalization?** Each row represents the attention scores of one query token against all key tokens in the input sequence. Applying softmax across the row converts these scores into attention weights that sum up to 1, indicating how much attention the query token assigns to each key token.\n\n   The attention weights are calculated using the softmax function:\n\n   $$\n   \\alpha_{ij} = \\frac{e^{s_{ij}}}{\\sum_{k=1}^{n} e^{s_{ik}}}\n   $$\n\n   Where:\n   - $s_{ij}$ = Attention score between query token $i$ and key token $j$\n   - $e$ = Base of the natural exponential function\n   - $n$ = Total number of tokens in the input sequence\n\n---\n\n![image.png](attachment:f798a67c-63b1-4bca-a863-8efec0c06f52.png)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-27T15:07:50.763353Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-27T15:07:50.763704Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-27T15:07:50.772158Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-27T15:07:50.763675Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-27T15:07:50.770991Z"}},"attachments":{"f798a67c-63b1-4bca-a863-8efec0c06f52.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Example: Computation of attention weight for query 2 i.e. journey\nattention_weight_2 = torch.softmax(attention_score_2,dim = 0) # softmax normalization\nprint(attention_weight_2)\nprint(attention_weight_2.sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.718567Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.718974Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.743207Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.71893Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.742323Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"tensor([0.1385, 0.2379, 0.2333, 0.1240, 0.1082, 0.1581])\ntensor(1.)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 3. Computation of Context Vector\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. **Weighted Sum of Input Vectors:** A context vector is the weighted sum of all input vectors, obtained by multiplying each input vector by its corresponding attention weight.\n\n   Mathematically,\n\n   $$ \n   \\mathbf{z}_i = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\alpha_{ij} \\mathbf{x}_j\n   $$\n\n   Where:\n\n   - $\\mathbf{z}_i$ = Context vector for token $i$\n   - $\\alpha_{ij}$ = Attention weight assigned by token $i$ to token $j$\n   - $\\mathbf{x}_j$ = Input vector of token $j$\n   - $n$ = Total number of tokens in the input sequence\n\n2. **Shape of the Context Vector:** Its shape is the same as the input vector.\n\n   If each input vector has dimension $d$, then:\n\n   $$\n   \\mathbf{x}_j \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d}\n   \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad\n   \\mathbf{z}_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d}\n   $$\n\n\n---\n\n![image.png](attachment:18eea829-0038-49f4-b20f-58548c3c0829.png)","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-27T15:12:14.930842Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-27T15:12:14.931203Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-27T15:12:14.939406Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-27T15:12:14.931154Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-27T15:12:14.938275Z"}},"attachments":{"18eea829-0038-49f4-b20f-58548c3c0829.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Example: Computing context vector for query 2\n\ncontext_vector_2 = torch.zeros(query.shape)\n\n\nfor idx,_ in enumerate(inputs):\n    context_vector_2 += attention_weight_2[idx] * inputs[idx]   # weighted sum\n\ncontext_vector_2","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.745583Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.745946Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.77047Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.745918Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.769677Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":6,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"tensor([0.4419, 0.6515, 0.5683])"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":6},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### General Formula for Computation of Context Vector\n\n#### 1. Computing Attention Scores\n\nConsider an input sequence consisting of **6 tokens**, where each token has an embedding dimension of **3**.\n\nTherefore, the input matrix has a shape of $(6, 3)$.\n\nIn self-attention, the input matrix is transformed into three matrices: **Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V)**.\n\nAssuming the Query and Key dimensions are 3:\n\n$$\nQ = XW_Q, \\qquad K = XW_K, \\qquad V = XW_V\n$$\n\nFor simplicity, assume all three matrices have a shape of $(6, 3)$.\n\n**Matrix shapes:**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\underbrace{X}_{6 \\times 3}\n\\quad\n\\underbrace{W_Q}_{3 \\times 3}\n\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad\n\\underbrace{Q}_{6 \\times 3}\n}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $K$ and $V$ also have shapes of $(6, 3)$.\n\nThe attention score matrix is computed by multiplying the Query matrix by the transpose of the Key matrix:\n\n$$\nS = QK^T\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{Q}_{6 \\times 3}\n\\ @ \\\n\\underbrace{K^T}_{3 \\times 6}\n\\ = \\\n\\underbrace{S}_{6 \\times 6}\n$$\n\nThe resulting attention score matrix has a shape of $(6, 6)$ because it contains one score for every query-key token pair.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Computing Attention Weights\n\nAttention weights are computed by applying the **softmax function row-wise** to the attention score matrix.\n\n$$\nA = \\operatorname{softmax}(S)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{S}_{6 \\times 6}\n\\quad \\xrightarrow{\\text{Row-wise Softmax}} \\quad\n\\underbrace{A}_{6 \\times 6}\n$$\n\nEach row of the attention weight matrix sums to 1:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{6} A_{ij} = 1\n$$\n\nEach row represents how a particular query token distributes its attention across all six key tokens.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Computing Context Vectors\n\nThe context vector matrix is calculated by multiplying the attention weight matrix by the Value matrix.\n\n$$\nC = AV\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A}_{6 \\times 6}\n\\ @ \\\n\\underbrace{V}_{6 \\times 3}\n\\ = \\\n\\underbrace{C}_{6 \\times 3}\n$$\n\nThe resulting context vector matrix has a shape of $(6, 3)$, which is the same as the Value matrix shape.\n\nEach row of $C$ represents the context vector for one token, computed as the weighted sum of all Value vectors using that token's attention weights.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Matrix Shapes\n\n| Matrix | Description | Shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| $X$ | Input embedding matrix | $(6, 3)$ |\n| $Q$ | Query matrix | $(6, 3)$ |\n| $K$ | Key matrix | $(6, 3)$ |\n| $V$ | Value matrix | $(6, 3)$ |\n| $K^T$ | Transpose of Key matrix | $(3, 6)$ |\n| $S = QK^T$ | Attention score matrix | $(6, 6)$ |\n| $A = \\operatorname{softmax}(S)$ | Attention weight matrix | $(6, 6)$ |\n| $C = AV$ | Context vector matrix | $(6, 3)$ |\n\n---\n\n","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-27T15:44:42.29985Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-27T15:44:42.300287Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-27T15:44:42.30821Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-27T15:44:42.30024Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-27T15:44:42.307073Z"}},"attachments":{"10b91ef9-805b-4867-9071-3e026ea1d739.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 1) Computing attention score\n\n# For simplicity consider inputs as Query,Key and Value vector\nQuery = inputs\nKey = inputs\nValue = inputs\n\n# computing attention score\natt_score = Query @ Key.T     # matrix multiplication\natt_score.shape,att_score","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.771492Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.772421Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.794712Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.772394Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.793924Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":7,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"(torch.Size([6, 6]),\n tensor([[0.9995, 0.9544, 0.9422, 0.4753, 0.4576, 0.6310],\n         [0.9544, 1.4950, 1.4754, 0.8434, 0.7070, 1.0865],\n         [0.9422, 1.4754, 1.4570, 0.8296, 0.7154, 1.0605],\n         [0.4753, 0.8434, 0.8296, 0.4937, 0.3474, 0.6565],\n         [0.4576, 0.7070, 0.7154, 0.3474, 0.6654, 0.2935],\n         [0.6310, 1.0865, 1.0605, 0.6565, 0.2935, 0.9450]]))"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":7},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 2) Computing attentin weight\n\natt_weight = torch.softmax(att_score,dim = -1)  # normalizing along the rows \natt_weight.shape,att_weight.sum(-1) # sum along each row\nprint(att_weight)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.795822Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.796527Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.805876Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.7965Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.804972Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"tensor([[0.2098, 0.2006, 0.1981, 0.1242, 0.1220, 0.1452],\n        [0.1385, 0.2379, 0.2333, 0.1240, 0.1082, 0.1581],\n        [0.1390, 0.2369, 0.2326, 0.1242, 0.1108, 0.1565],\n        [0.1435, 0.2074, 0.2046, 0.1462, 0.1263, 0.1720],\n        [0.1526, 0.1958, 0.1975, 0.1367, 0.1879, 0.1295],\n        [0.1385, 0.2184, 0.2128, 0.1420, 0.0988, 0.1896]])\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 3) Computing Context vector\n\ncontext_vector = att_weight @ Value # Matrix Multiplicatoin\ncontext_vector  # each row in the context vector matrix represent contex vector of each token at it's position ","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.807057Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.807524Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.822883Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.807494Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.821934Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":9,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"tensor([[0.4421, 0.5931, 0.5790],\n        [0.4419, 0.6515, 0.5683],\n        [0.4431, 0.6496, 0.5671],\n        [0.4304, 0.6298, 0.5510],\n        [0.4671, 0.5910, 0.5266],\n        [0.4177, 0.6503, 0.5645]])"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Implementing self-attention with trainable weights","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-27T17:05:46.872858Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-27T17:05:46.87326Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-27T17:05:46.880071Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-27T17:05:46.873228Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-27T17:05:46.879066Z"}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q.4) Why Do We Need Trainable Weight Matrices in Self-Attention?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. **Learning Good Context Vectors:** Trainable weight matrices are crucial because they allow the model to learn how to produce **good context vectors** by learning meaningful Query, Key, and Value representations from the input embeddings.\n\n---\n\n![image.png](attachment:14e579bf-6aea-4474-a40d-e073d46879fb.png)","metadata":{},"attachments":{"14e579bf-6aea-4474-a40d-e073d46879fb.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#### Implementing a compact self-attention class `selfAttention_v1`","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-27T18:04:10.415437Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-27T18:04:10.41582Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-27T18:04:12.506314Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-27T18:04:10.415785Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-27T18:04:12.505262Z"}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# selfAttention_v1 class\n\nclass selfAttention_v1(torch.nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self,d_in,d_out):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.W_query = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.rand(d_in,d_out))\n        self.W_key = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.rand(d_in,d_out))\n        self.W_value = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.rand(d_in,d_out))\n\n    def forward(self,x):\n        query = x @ self.W_query\n        key = x @ self.W_key\n        value = x @ self.W_value\n\n        # Step-1) Attention score\n        att_score = query @ key.T   \n        # Step-2) Attention weight\n        att_weight = torch.softmax(att_score / key.shape[-1] ** 0.5,dim = -1)  # scaled normalization\n        # Step-3) Context vector\n        context_vectors = att_weight @ value\n        return context_vectors","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.824316Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.824642Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.838313Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.824616Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.837339Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# calling selfAttention_v1 class\ntorch.manual_seed(123)\nd_in = 3\nd_out = 2\nsa_v1 = selfAttention_v1(d_in,d_out)\ncontext_vec1 = sa_v1(inputs)\ncontext_vec1","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.839443Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.839818Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.877433Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.839784Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T15:45:31.876515Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":11,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"tensor([[0.2996, 0.8053],\n        [0.3061, 0.8210],\n        [0.3058, 0.8203],\n        [0.2948, 0.7939],\n        [0.2927, 0.7891],\n        [0.2990, 0.8040]], grad_fn=<MmBackward0>)"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q.5) Why Do We Need to Scale the Dot Product While Computing Attention Weights?\n\n**Answer:**\n\n1. **Improving Training Stability:** We scale the dot product to prevent the softmax function from producing extremely small gradients, which can slow down learning during backpropagation.\n\n2. **Effect of Large Embedding Dimensions:** When the dimensions of Query and Key vectors are large, their dot products can become large in magnitude. This can cause the softmax function to produce attention weights that are close to 0 or 1.\n\n3. **Effect on Gradients:** When softmax outputs are close to 0 or 1, its gradients can become very small. As a result, the model may learn slowly because the weight matrices receive weaker gradient updates.\n\n4. **Scaling by the Square Root of the Dimension:** To reduce this problem, we divide the Query-Key dot product by the square root of the key vector dimension. This mechanism is called **Scaled Dot-Product Attention**.\n\n   **Formula:**\n\n   $$\n   \\text{Attention Scores} = \\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\n   $$\n\n   Where:\n   - $Q$ = Query matrix\n   - $K^T$ = Transpose of the Key matrix\n   - $d_k$ = Dimension of each Key vector\n   - $\\sqrt{d_k}$ = Scaling factor\n\n   The scaled attention scores are then passed through softmax to obtain the attention weights:\n\n   $$\n   A = \\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right)\n   $$\n\n**Key Takeaway:** Scaling keeps attention scores at a more manageable magnitude, helping softmax avoid saturation and allowing gradients to flow more effectively during training.\n\n","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-28T11:58:15.196085Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-28T11:58:15.196884Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-28T11:58:15.216979Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-28T11:58:15.19685Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-28T11:58:15.216092Z"}},"attachments":{"08c98a2c-5016-4191-b708-fda3412051b9.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import torch\n\n# Problem: Large embedding dimensions can produce large attention scores